      "math_academy_id": "q-78961",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6c987d6e-0dd9-50fa-85a0-905c93e07413",
      "knowledge_point": "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing an Arbitrary Logarithm",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int 16{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=16{x}^{3}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int 16{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=4{x}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 16{x}^{3}{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(x)(4{x}^{4})-\\int \\frac{4{x}^{4}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}{log}_{3}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{4}{log}_{3}⁡(x)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\cdot {x}^{4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$",
