    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-78973",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759",
      "knowledge_point": "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor",
      "problem": "$\\int 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int 2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x=2\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡4x\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2[uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =2[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)-\\int \\,\\text{d}x] \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)-2x+C \\\\  & =2x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)-1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integral constant.",
