    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-114592",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4435e346-d6ad-58cd-97cb-bbd45e121759",
      "knowledge_point": "Using Integration by Parts to Compute an Integral Containing a Single Logarithmic Factor",
      "problem": "$\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe will solve this problem using integration by parts. We start by defining $u$ and ${v}^{′},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=\\frac{1}{x-3} \\\\ {v}^{′}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\,{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\cdot x-\\int x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\frac{x}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use a substitution $x-3=u,$ thus $x=u+3$ and $\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\frac{x}{x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\frac{u+3}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-\\int \\,\\text{d}u-3\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-u-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡u+D \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-(x-3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+D \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-x+3+D \\\\  & =(x-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=D+3$ is an integral constant.",
